package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneTerrainFoliage is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/Scenes/terrainFoliage.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Learn to add foliage to a terrain in Quorum Studio, paint and remove it with a brush, and measure how it changes a game's frame rate.")

        Title("Foliage")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Bare ground looks empty. Real land is covered with rocks, bushes, trees and
grass, and placing each one by hand would take forever. In Quorum Studio,
these are called "):Term("foliage"):Text(": models or pictures that the terrain scatters
over the ground in many copies. You choose which ones a terrain can use, and
then paint them on with a brush, the way you paint ground materials. In this
tutorial, you will add rocks to a terrain, paint them, remove them, and
change their settings. You will also learn why thousands of copies can slow
a game down, and which settings keep it running smoothly.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Add a foliage asset to a terrain, and paint and remove its copies with
the Foliage tool")
        ListItem("Change an asset's settings, such as its density, size, lean, color,
wind, levels of detail and shadows")
        ListItem("Explain what foliage costs the computer each frame, and how density and
draw distance change the frame rate")
        ListItem("Compare two versions of a scene and decide which one plays better")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Imagine drawing a forest by hand, one tree at a time, and then being asked
to draw it again 60 times every second. What shortcuts would you take? Would
you draw the trees far away as carefully as the ones close to you? Would you
draw the ones behind you at all?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_2_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHITECTURE_6)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_2_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_PLAYER_EXPERIENCE_5)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_DATA_SCIENCE_CREATION_AND_CURATION_1)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_HARDWARE_AND_SOFTWARE_30)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Paragraph("This tutorial uses a terrain from the earlier tutorials, with some hills and
flat places. Select it before you start, from the scene tree or by clicking
the ground. Like the terrain itself, foliage is drawn with Vulkan.")
        GettingAModel()
        AddingAnAsset()
        PaintingFoliage()
        RemovingFoliage()
        FoliageFollowsTheGround()
        AnAssetsSettings()
        WhatFoliageCosts()
        MeasuringTheFrameRate()
    end

    private action GettingAModel
        Subsection("Getting a model")
        Paragraph("Foliage starts with a model file. Quorum Studio can use models in the
"):Code(".glb"):Text(", "):Code(".gltf"):Text(", "):Code(".fbx"):Text(" and "):Code(".obj"):Text(" formats, or a "):Code(".png"):Text(" picture for a card.
Small, simple models work best, because there will be many copies of them.")
        Paragraph("The examples in this tutorial use three rock models, "):Code("RockA.glb"):Text(",
"):Code("RockB.glb"):Text(" and "):Code("RockC.glb"):Text(", from the demo of Terrain3D, a free terrain tool
for the Godot game engine. They are shared under the MIT license, which
lets anyone use them as long as the license notice goes with them. Put the
models in a folder in your project, such as "):Code("Models/Foliage"):Text(", with the
license file beside them. As with any art you didn't make, add a line to
your game's credits saying where the rocks came from.")
    end

    private action AddingAnAsset
        Subsection("Adding an asset")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Select the terrain, and go to the "):Strong("Foliage"):Text(" section of the Properties
panel. With the keyboard, press "):Keys("Ctrl", "P"):Text(" ("):Keys("Cmd", "P"):Text(" on Mac) and "):Key("Tab"):Text(" until
you reach it.")
        ListItem("Press "):Strong("Add asset..."):Text(", and choose a model or a picture.")
        ListItem("Quorum Studio reports the change to accessibility tools, which may give
a phrase such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Added RockA as foliage asset 1, a model. Paint it with
the Foliage tool."):DoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("The first time you add an asset, Quorum Studio makes a "):Term("foliage list"):Text(" for
the terrain: a file in the project's "):Code("Scenes/Foliage"):Text(" folder, named after
the terrain's folder, such as "):Code("Scenes/Foliage/Terrain1.json"):Text(". The list
holds the assets and their settings. The copies themselves are saved beside
the terrain's own files, one file for each region that has foliage.")
        Paragraph("The section's first line says how many copies the terrain has in all, such
as "):DoubleQuote():Output("20719 copies in all."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Under it, the assets are listed in alphabetical
order. Each asset is a heading that opens and closes, like the other parts
of the Properties panel, with a line under it that says whether it is a
model or a card and how many copies it has, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Card, 20529 copies"):DoubleQuote():Text(".
Click an asset's name or its arrow to open it, or move to it with "):Key("Tab"):Text("
and press "):Key("Enter"):Text("; the same opens and closes each group of settings. Only
one asset is open at a time.")
        Paragraph("An open asset shows:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Name:"):Text(" what the asset is called in the section and in Quorum Studio's
reports.")
        ListItem():Strong("Its settings,"):Text(" in five groups that open and close: Placement, Lean
and turn, Color and wind, Levels of detail, and Shadows. They are
described below.")
        ListItem():Strong("Remove"):Text(" (for example, "):Quoted("Remove Grass"):Text("), which takes the asset away with
every copy of it. This can't be undone, so take care with it.")
        Paragraph("Adding and removing assets can't be undone yet.")
        // Image to add: /media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/FoliageSection.png — The Foliage section listing rock and grass assets, with Grass open and its Color and wind settings showing.
    end

    private action PaintingFoliage
        Subsection("Painting foliage")
        Paragraph("The "):Strong("Foliage"):Text(" tool paints where one asset grows: its "):Term("coverage"):Text(", from
none to full. Quorum Studio then works out the copies from the coverage and
the asset's settings.")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Choose the Foliage tool: press "):Keys("Shift", "Alt", "4"):Text(" ("):Keys("Shift", "Option", "4"):Text(" on Mac),
or choose "):Strong("Foliage"):Text(" in the Brush section's Tool group. You can also
find it in the "):Strong("Scenes"):Text(" menu ("):Keys("Alt", "C"):Text(", or "):Keys("Option", "C"):Text(" on Mac), under
"):Strong("Terrain"):Text(" and then "):Strong("Tools"):Text(".")
        ListItem("Choose the asset to paint in the Brush section's "):Strong("Foliage Asset"):Text("
card. It lists the terrain's assets in the same alphabetical order as
the Foliage section.")
        ListItem("Set the brush. "):Strong("Size"):Text(" is the brush's radius. "):Strong("Strength"):Text(" sets how
thick the asset grows where you paint: the usual strength or more is the
asset's full density, and less is sparser. Painting a weaker brush over
thick growth thins it. Press "):Keys("Alt", "="):Text(" and "):Keys("Alt", "-"):Text(" for a larger or smaller brush, and
"):Keys("Alt", "]"):Text(" and "):Keys("Alt", "["):Text(" for a stronger or weaker one (on Mac, "):Key("Option"):Text(").")
        ListItem("Paint:")
        NestedBulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("With the keyboard:"):Text(" move the brush with the arrow keys and hold
"):Key("Space"):Text(". Keep holding it and move the brush to paint a trail.")
        ListItem():Strong("With the mouse:"):Text(" a gray ring follows the pointer, showing where the
brush would go. Click outside the brush's ring to move the brush
there, and click inside it to paint once. Hold the button down for a
moment, anywhere, to start painting there, and drag to keep painting.")
        EndNestedList()
        Paragraph("Under the brush, the coverage moves toward the brush's strength: fully near
the middle, fading toward the edge. Copies grow at random spots within the
coverage, with random sizes and turns, but only where the ground's slope
suits the asset. The same coverage always grows the same copies, so the
foliage is the same every time the scene opens, and in the running game.
When you let go, Quorum Studio reports what changed and what is now under
the brush, as a phrase such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Added 14 RockA. Under the brush: 14 RockA, coverage 1.0."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Painting a weaker brush over thick growth reports, for
example, "):DoubleQuote():Output("Thinned RockA, 40 fewer."):DoubleQuote():Text(" The whole stroke is one step for Undo.")
        Paragraph("If the terrain has no assets yet, the report is "):DoubleQuote():Output("The Foliage tool needs a
foliage asset. Add one in the Foliage section."):DoubleQuote()
        // Image to add: /media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/PaintedRocks.png — Gray rocks scattered over a grassy hill, with the brush's ring among them.
    end

    private action RemovingFoliage
        Subsection("Removing foliage")
        Paragraph("The same tool removes foliage, by lowering its coverage. Hold a key down "):Strong("as the stroke starts"):Text(":")
        Table("Keys for foliage", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/Keys.csv")
        Paragraph("With the mouse, hold "):Key("Ctrl"):Text(" while you start the stroke. On a Mac, use the
"):Key("Control"):Text(" key, not "):Key("Command"):Text(". Because Control and a click is a right click
on a Mac, press "):Key("Control"):Text(" first and then hold "):Key("Space"):Text(". Quorum Studio reports
how many copies went, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Removed 5 RockA."):DoubleQuote():Text(" When every asset is
removed, the report names "):Quoted("foliage"):Text(" instead of an asset's name.")
    end

    private action FoliageFollowsTheGround
        Subsection("Foliage follows the ground")
        Paragraph("You can keep shaping the ground after painting foliage. When you raise or
lower the ground, stamp a hill, erode it, or undo a change, the copies move
up or down with the ground, so rocks never float in the air or sink out of
sight. When a stroke ends, the copies are worked out again, so an asset
still grows only on the slopes it suits. Nothing needs to be turned on for
this; it always happens.")
    end

    private action AnAssetsSettings
        Subsection("An asset's settings")
        Paragraph("Open an asset in the Foliage section to change how its copies are placed
and drawn. A field takes its new value when you leave it, with "):Key("Tab"):Text(" or a
click elsewhere. A value that isn't a number, or is out of range, is
ignored.")
        Paragraph("Every setting applies to "):Strong("all"):Text(" of the asset's copies, everywhere you
painted it, as soon as you change it. You never need to paint again to
change how foliage looks. Raising "):Strong("Density"):Text(" keeps every copy that is
there and adds more between them; lowering it takes some away, and the rest
stay where they are.")

        Subsubsection("Placement")
        Table("Placement settings", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/SettingsPlacement.csv")

        Subsubsection("Lean and turn")
        Table("Lean and turn settings", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/SettingsLean.csv")

        Subsubsection("Color and wind")
        Table("Color and wind settings", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/SettingsColor.csv")

        Subsubsection("Levels of detail")
        Paragraph("Far from the camera, a detailed model is only a few pixels tall, so
drawing all its triangles is wasted work. "):Term("Levels of detail"):Text(" let a copy
switch to something simpler when it's far away. The asset's own model or
picture is used up close. Each level after it is used from a distance you
choose, and the farthest level that has been reached is the one drawn.
Levels are chosen for a whole cell of foliage at a time, measured from the
camera to the cell's nearest side.")
        Table("Levels of detail settings", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/SettingsLevels.csv")
        Paragraph("For example, a tree might use its full model up close, a simpler model
from 40 units, a flat card from 100 units, and nothing past 250.")

        Subsubsection("Shadows")
        Table("Shadow settings", "media/scenes/Terrain/Foliage/SettingsShadows.csv")
    end

    private action WhatFoliageCosts
        Subsection("What foliage costs")
        Paragraph("Every copy of a rock is drawn from the same model, but each copy is still
real work for the computer, and the work is split between two of its parts,
which work in very different ways. The "):Term("Central Processing Unit (CPU)"):Text("
runs your program. It follows the program's steps one after another, very
quickly, and makes the decisions, such as what the player pressed and what
should happen next. Even a fast CPU has only a handful of cores, each doing
one thing at a time. The "):Term("Graphics Processing Unit (GPU)"):Text(" is a separate
processor built for one job: working out pictures. It has thousands of small
cores that all do the same simple calculation at the same time, each on a
different triangle or point of the screen. A CPU is like a few expert chefs
who can cook anything, one dish at a time. A GPU is like a thousand kitchen
helpers who can each only chop, but all chop at once. So the CPU decides
what to draw, and the GPU draws it.")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("The GPU draws the triangles."):Text(" A model is made of small flat
triangles. A rock might have a few hundred. A thousand copies of that
rock means a few hundred thousand triangles, and the GPU has to draw
them all again on every frame. A detailed tree can have many thousands of
triangles, so a forest adds up very fast. Shadows cost more: the GPU
draws every copy that casts a shadow a second time, from the light's
point of view.")
        ListItem():Strong("The CPU prepares and culls."):Text(" Before the GPU can draw, the program has
to decide what to draw. Each frame, it checks which copies are close
enough and in front of the camera, and skips the rest. That skipping is
called "):Term("culling"):Text(". The more copies there are, the more there is to
check.")
        Paragraph("A game has very little time for all of this. At 60 frames a second, each
frame has about 16 thousandths of a second for everything: the player's
input, the physics, the terrain, the water and the foliage. If drawing
takes longer than that, the game draws fewer frames each second, and
movement starts to look jerky.")
        Paragraph("Quorum's terrain uses a few tricks to keep foliage fast:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Cells."):Text(" The ground is split into squares about 32 units wide, and each
square holds one asset's copies. The program culls a whole cell at once,
instead of checking every copy one at a time. Cells of foliage that
casts no shadows, such as grass, are skipped when they're outside the
camera's view, so grass behind the player costs nothing. Cells that
cast shadows are kept, since a tree just out of view can still throw
its shadow into it.")
        ListItem():Strong("Drawing copies together."):Text(" All the copies in a cell are sent to the GPU
in one go, which the GPU can draw much faster than the same copies sent
one by one. The copies are worked out once and kept ready, so on each
frame the CPU only copies them over, instead of working out every copy
again.")
        ListItem():Strong("Draw distance."):Text(" Cells farther away than the asset's draw distance
aren't drawn at all, and copies thin out near that distance, so they
fade out gradually instead of all vanishing at once.")
        ListItem():Strong("Levels of detail."):Text(" Far away, an asset can switch to a simpler model or
a flat card, with far fewer triangles. Up close, it looks the same.")
        ListItem():Strong("Cheaper shadows."):Text(" Far levels can skip shadows, and a simple level can
cast the shadow for a detailed one.")
        Paragraph("So two settings matter most for speed. "):Strong("Density"):Text(", with the coverage you
painted, decides how many copies there are in all. "):Strong("Draw distance"):Text(" decides how many of them are drawn on
each frame. Halving the draw distance leaves only about a quarter of the
area around the camera to draw, because the area of a circle grows with
the square of its radius.")
    end

    private action MeasuringTheFrameRate
        Subsection("Measuring the frame rate")
        Paragraph("A game can count its own frames. Quorum calls a game's "):Code("Update"):Text(" action once
every frame, and its "):Code("seconds"):Text(" value says how long the last frame took.
This program loads your scene and counts the frames. Once a second, it
writes the count to Quorum Studio's output window, and also adds it as a
line to a "):Term("log file"):Text(", so you can study the whole run afterward as data.
Put it in your project's "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(", and change the scene's path if yours
is different.")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/SceneTerrainFoliage/FoliageFrames.quorum")
        Paragraph("The log is a small table in a format called "):Term("CSV"):Text(", for comma-separated
values. Its first line names the columns, "):Code("second,frames"):Text(", and each line
after it is one second of the run, such as "):Code("12,58"):Text(": in the twelfth second,
the game drew 58 frames. You can open it in a spreadsheet, or read it in
any text editor or with a screen reader, one line at a time. "):Code("PushToDisk"):Text("
saves each line as soon as it's written, so nothing is lost if the game
stops suddenly. "):Code("OnExit"):Text(" is called when the game's window closes, and it
closes the file. Taking away 1 from "):Code("elapsed"):Text(", instead of setting it to 0,
keeps the bit of time left over, so every line covers a full second.")
        Paragraph("Games usually limit how many frames they draw a second, often to about 60
or 70, so a fast computer may show the same number with or without
foliage. The number matters when it drops below that limit.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("The goal of this activity is to find out, with real numbers, how much
foliage a scene can hold before the game slows down, and to decide which
of two versions plays better. Work in pairs, with one computer. First,
agree on a test: the same scene, the same camera, and the frame-logging
program above. For each run, let the game run for 30 seconds without
touching anything, then close its window. Give each run its own log name:
"):Code("FramesNone.csv"):Text(", "):Code("FramesA.csv"):Text(" and "):Code("FramesB.csv"):Text(".")
        Paragraph("Run it with no foliage. Then make "):Strong("Version A"):Text(": paint rocks over a large
area at full strength, set a high density and a long draw distance, such
as 400, and run it. Then make "):Strong("Version B"):Text(" from the same painting: lower
the density, shorten the draw distance, such as 100, and remove the rocks
far from where the player starts, so they stay only where a player would
notice them. Run it once more. If your computer is fast enough that
nothing changes, keep making Version A denser until the frame rate drops.
If you can, also run Version A on a second, different computer, and keep
that log too.")
        Paragraph("Compare the versions using your logs. Write a short report that answers
these questions:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("How many copies did each version have? The Foliage section's first line
gives the total.")
        ListItem("From each log, what were the average, lowest and highest frames per
second? How many seconds were below 60?")
        ListItem("Did the frame rate stay steady, or were there sudden drops? Which rows of
the log show it?")
        ListItem("Move the brush to where the player starts, choose the Foliage tool, and
read what's under the brush in each version ("):DoubleQuote():Output("Under the brush: 14
RockA"):DoubleQuote():Text("). Did Version B remove copies near the player, or far away? Why is
far away the cheaper place to cut?")
        ListItem("If you ran Version A on a second computer, how did its log differ? What
about that computer might explain it?")
        ListItem("Which version would you ship, and why? What would you change for a
player with an older or slower computer?")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Foliage turns bare ground into a place, but every copy is work for the CPU
and the GPU on every frame. Density, draw distance, shadows and simpler far
models are the levers that trade looks for speed. Professional games make
the same trade, and many let players choose between "):Quoted("high"):Text(" and "):Quoted("low"):Text("
settings. In the next tutorial, "):Link("Lakes", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainLakes.html"):Text(",
you will fill the hollows in your terrain with water.")
    end
end
